Disable mysql_clear_password plugin by default (GHSA-3f6p-5ww8-9rcr)

The mysql_clear_password authentication plugin sends passwords in plaintext,
which is a security risk when the connection is not encrypted. A malicious
server (e.g. via DNS poisoning) could request cleartext auth via an
AuthSwitchRequest and capture credentials.

This backport gates the built-in mysql_clear_password plugin behind an explicit
opt-in (the new `enableCleartextPlugin` option, or a user-supplied custom
`mysql_clear_password` entry in `authPlugins`). When the server requests
mysql_clear_password and it is not enabled, the connection fails with a
`MYSQL_CLEAR_PASSWORD_NOT_ENABLED` error. The client_handshake error handler is
also updated to preserve an existing `err.code` instead of unconditionally
overwriting it with `AUTH_SWITCH_PLUGIN_ERROR`.

Upstream fix: 884bec56288d827939d0dd3f1f4ae476fbc8dbeb

